r}[X]=2,$ $\\text{Var}[Y]=4,$ and $\\text{Cov}[X,Y]=3.$ Find $\\text{Var}[X+2Y].$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527126 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "079f02ad-9ee0-58ac-bead-d4394104b125" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527126 #edb/ref 1074 "Recall that if $X$ and $Y$ are random variables, and $a$ and $b$ are constants, then\n\n$$\n\\text{Var}[aX+bY]={a}^{2}\\text{Var}[X]+{b}^{2}\\text{Var}[Y]+2ab\\,\\text{Cov}[X,Y].\n$$\n\nSubstituting the given data into the formula above, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[X+2Y] & ={1}^{2}\\cdot 2+{2}^{2}\\cdot 4+2\\cdot 1\\cdot 2\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =2+16+12 \\\\  & =30.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527134 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186527086 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527134 #edb/ref 1069 "q-148330" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527134 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527134 #edb/ref 1071 "For two random variables $X$ and $Y,$ we have $\\text{Var}[X]=0.24,$ $\\text{Var}[Y]=0.06,$ and $\\text{Cov}[X,Y]=0.08.$ Find $\\text{Var}[2X-8Y].$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527134 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "1e6dcf9d-d6cd-51ac-b73d-58cf9701a199" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527134 #edb/ref 1074 "Recall that if $X$ and $Y$ are random variables, and $a$ and $b$ are constants, then\n\n$$\n\\text{Var}[aX+bY]={a}^{2}\\text{Var}[X]+{b}^{2}\\text{Var}[Y]+2ab\\,\\text{Cov}[X,Y].\n$$\n\nSubstituting the given data into the formula above, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[2X-8Y] & ={2}^{2}\\cdot 0.24+(-8)^{2}\\cdot 0.06+2\\cdot 2\\cdot (-8)\\cdot 0.08 \\\\  & =0.96+3.84-2.56 \\\\  & =2.24.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527156 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186527032 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527156 #edb/ref 1069 "q-148346" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527156 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527156 #edb/ref 1071 "The joint probability mass function of the random variables $X$ and $Y$ is given below.\n\n| $f$ | $Y=0$ | $Y=1$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=0$ | $0.1$ | $0.2$ |\n| $X=2$ | $0.4$ | $0.3$ |\n\nFind $\\text{Var}[3X-Y].$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527156 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "43034259-a22b-5dd7-b48f-a2ed42e0b13d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527156 #edb/ref 1074 "Recall that if $X$ and $Y$ are random variables and $a$ and $b$ are constants, then\n\n$$\n\\text{Var}[aX+bY]={a}^{2}\\text{Var}[X]+{b}^{2}\\text{Var}[Y]+2ab\\,\\text{Cov}[X,Y].\n$$\n\nAlso, the variance of a random variable $X$ is determined by the formula\n\n$$\n\\text{Var}[X]=\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]-\\text{E}[X]^{2},\n$$\n\nand the covariance of two random variables $X$ and $Y$ is given by the formula\n\n$$\n\\text{Cov}[X,Y]=\\text{E}[XY]-\\text{E}[X]\\cdot \\text{E}[Y].\n$$\n\nFirst, let's find the marginal mass functions for $X$ and $Y.$ These are given by the row and column totals, respectively.\n\n| $f$ | $Y=0$ | $Y=1$ | ${f}_{X}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=0$ | $0.1$ | $0.2$ | $0.3$ |\n| $X=2$ | $0.4$ | $0.3$ | $0.7$ |\n| ${f}_{Y}$ | $0.5$ | $0.5$ | $1$ |\n\nNow, we can find $\\text{Var}[X].$ If $X$ is defined on a sample space ${S}_{X},$ then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[X] & ={\\sum }_{x\\in {S}_{X}}x\\cdot {f}_{X}(x) \\\\  & =0\\cdot 0.3+2\\cdot 0.7 \\\\  & =1.4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[{X}^{2}] & ={\\sum }_{x\\in {S}_{X}}{x}^{2}\\cdot {f}_{X}(x) \\\\  & ={0}^{2}\\cdot 0.3+{2}^{2}\\cdot 0.7 \\\\  & =2.8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[X] & =\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]-\\text{E}[X]^{2} \\\\  & =2.8-{1.4}^{2} \\\\  & =0.84.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, if $Y$ is defined on a sample space ${S}_{Y},$ then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[Y] & ={\\sum }_{y\\in {S}_{y}}y\\cdot {f}_{Y}(y) \\\\  & =0\\cdot 0.5+1\\cdot 0.5 \\\\  & =0.5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[{Y}^{2}] & ={\\sum }_{y\\in {S}_{y}}{y}^{2}\\cdot {f}_{Y}(y) \\\\  & ={0}^{2}\\cdot 0.5+{1}^{2}\\cdot 0.5 \\\\  & =0.5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[Y] & =\\text{E}[{Y}^{2}]-\\text{E}[Y]^{2} \\\\  & =0.5-{0.5}^{2} \\\\  & =0.25.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's find $\\text{Cov}[X,Y].$ We find $\\text{E}[XY]$ using the rule of the lazy statistician:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[XY] & ={\\sum }_{(x,y)\\in S}xy\\cdot f(x,y) \\\\  & =0\\cdot 0\\cdot f(0,0)+0\\cdot 1\\cdot f(0,1)+2\\cdot 0\\cdot f(2,0)+2\\cdot 1\\cdot f(2,1) \\\\  & =0+0+0+2\\cdot 0.3 \\\\  & =0.6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, using the covariance formula, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Cov}[XY] & =\\text{E}[XY]-\\text{E}[X]\\cdot \\text{E}[Y] \\\\  & =0.6-1.4\\cdot 0.5 \\\\  & =-0.1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[3X-Y] & ={3}^{2}\\cdot 0.84+(-1)^{2}\\cdot 0.25+2\\cdot 3\\cdot (-1)\\cdot (-0.1) \\\\  & =7.56+0.25+0.6 \\\\  & =8.41.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527173 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186527083 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527173 #edb/ref 1069 "q-148351" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527173 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527173 #edb/ref 1071 "The joint probability mass function of the random variables $X$ and $Y$ is given below.\n\n| $f$ | $Y=1$ | $Y=2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=0$ | $0.3$ | $0.15$ |\n| $X=4$ | $0.2$ | $0.35$ |\n\nFind $\\text{Var}[3X+4Y].$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527173 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "7c291da1-e168-5e62-8128-2d4c3d94af53" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527173 #edb/ref 1074 "Recall that if $X$ and $Y$ are random variables and $a$ and $b$ are constants, then\n\n$$\n\\text{Var}[aX+bY]={a}^{2}\\text{Var}[X]+{b}^{2}\\text{Var}[Y]+2ab\\,\\text{Cov}[X,Y].\n$$\n\nAlso, the variance of a random variable $X$ is determined by the formula\n\n$$\n\\text{Var}[X]=\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]-\\text{E}[X]^{2},\n$$\n\nand the covariance of two random variables $X$ and $Y$ is given by the formula\n\n$$\n\\text{Cov}[X,Y]=\\text{E}[XY]-\\text{E}[X]\\cdot \\text{E}[Y].\n$$\n\nFirst, let's find the marginal mass functions for $X$ and $Y.$ These are given by the row and column totals, respectively.\n\n| $f$ | $Y=1$ | $Y=2$ | ${f}_{X}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=0$ | $0.3$ | $0.15$ | $0.45$ |\n| $X=4$ | $0.2$ | $0.35$ | $0.55$ |\n| ${f}_{Y}$ | $0.5$ | $0.5$ | $1$ |\n\nNow, we can find $\\text{Var}[X].$ If $X$ is defined on a sample space ${S}_{X},$ then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[X] & ={\\sum }_{x\\in {S}_{X}}x\\cdot {f}_{X}(x) \\\\  & =0\\cdot 0.45+4\\cdot 0.55 \\\\  & =2.2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[{X}^{2}] & ={\\sum }_{x\\in {S}_{X}}{x}^{2}\\cdot {f}_{X}(x) \\\\  & ={0}^{2}\\cdot 0.45+{4}^{2}\\cdot 0.55 \\\\  & =8.8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[X] & =\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]-\\text{E}[X]^{2} \\\\  & =8.8-{2.2}^{2} \\\\  & =3.96.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, if $Y$ is defined on a sample space ${S}_{Y},$ then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[Y] & ={\\sum }_{y\\in {S}_{y}}y\\cdot {f}_{Y}(y) \\\\  & =1\\cdot 0.5+2\\cdot 0.5 \\\\  & =1.5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[{Y}^{2}] & ={\\sum }_{y\\in {S}_{y}}{y}^{2}\\cdot {f}_{Y}(y) \\\\  & ={1}^{2}\\cdot 0.5+{2}^{2}\\cdot 0.5 \\\\  & =2.5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[Y] & =\\text{E}[{Y}^{2}]-\\text{E}[Y]^{2} \\\\  & =2.5-{1.5}^{2} \\\\  & =0.25.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's find $\\text{Cov}[X,Y].$ We find $\\text{E}[XY]$ using the rule of the lazy statistician:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[XY] & ={\\sum }_{(x,y)\\in S}xy\\cdot f(x,y) \\\\  & =0\\cdot 1\\cdot f(0,1)+0\\cdot 2\\cdot f(0,2)+4\\cdot 1\\cdot f(4,1)+4\\cdot 2\\cdot f(4,2) \\\\  & =0+0+4\\cdot 0.2+8\\cdot 0.35 \\\\  & =3.6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, using the covariance formula, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Cov}[XY] & =\\text{E}[XY]-\\text{E}[X]\\cdot \\text{E}[Y] \\\\  & =3.6-2.2\\cdot 1.5 \\\\  & =0.3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[3X+4Y] & ={3}^{2}\\cdot 3.96+{4}^{2}\\cdot 0.25+2\\cdot 3\\cdot 4\\cdot 0.3 \\\\  & =35.64+4+7.2 \\\\  & =46.84.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527183 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186527036 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527183 #edb/ref 1069 "q-148315" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527183 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527183 #edb/ref 1071 "For two random variables $X$ and $Y,$ we have $\\text{Var}[X]=\\frac{7}{4},$ $\\text{Var}[Y]=\\frac{5}{2},$ and $\\text{Cov}[X,Y]=\\frac{3}{4}.$ Find $\\text{Var}[X-Y].$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527183 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "94546e94-1af9-54e7-9a1b-29fb0570cfc8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527183 #edb/ref 1074 "Recall that if $X$ and $Y$ are random variables, then\n\n$$\n\\text{Var}[X\\pm Y]=\\text{Var}[X]+\\text{Var}[Y]\\pm 2\\text{Cov}[X,Y].\n$$\n\nSubstituting the given data into the formula above, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\text{Var}[X-Y]=\\frac{7}{4}+\\frac{5}{2}-2\\cdot \\frac{3}{4}=\\frac{11}{4}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527189 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186527078 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527189 #edb/ref 1069 "q-148310" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527189 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527189 #edb/ref 1071 "For two random variables $X$ and $Y,$ we have $\\text{Var}[X]=4,$ $\\text{Var}[Y]=1,$ and $\\text{Cov}[X,Y]=1.2.$ Find $\\text{Var}[X-Y].$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527189 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "a0026095-8676-593f-8d69-3fa08a38ef1b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527189 #edb/ref 1074 "Recall that if $X$ and $Y$ are random variables, then\n\n$$\n\\text{Var}[X\\pm Y]=\\text{Var}[X]+\\text{Var}[Y]\\pm 2\\text{Cov}[X,Y].\n$$\n\nSubstituting the given data into the formula above, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\text{Var}[X-Y]=4+1-2\\cdot 1.2=2.6.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527198 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186527066 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527198 #edb/ref 1069 "q-148331" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527198 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527198 #edb/ref 1071 "For two random variables $X$ and $Y,$ we have $\\text{Var}[X]=0.1,$ $\\text{Var}[Y]=0.7,$ and $\\text{Cov}[X,Y]=0.2.$ Find $\\text{Var}[7X-4Y].$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527198 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "af828237-c44f-5a8f-92ae-a55b1aff2b6f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527198 #edb/ref 1074 "Recall that if $X$ and $Y$ are random variables, and $a$ and $b$ are constants, then\n\n$$\n\\text{Var}[aX+bY]={a}^{2}\\text{Var}[X]+{b}^{2}\\text{Var}[Y]+2ab\\,\\text{Cov}[X,Y].\n$$\n\nSubstituting the given data into the formula above, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[7X-4Y] & ={7}^{2}\\cdot 0.1+(-4)^{2}\\cdot 0.7+2\\cdot 7\\cdot (-4)\\cdot 0.2 \\\\  & =4.9+11.2-11.2 \\\\  & =4.9.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527205 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186527103 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527205 #edb/ref 1069 "q-148307" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527205 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527205 #edb/ref 1071 "For two random variables $X$ and $Y,$ we have $\\text{Var}[X]=\\frac{1}{8},$ $\\text{Var}[Y]=\\frac{3}{4},$ and $\\text{Cov}[X,Y]=\\frac{5}{16}.$ Find $\\text{Var}[X+Y].$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527205 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "bade78ae-2b02-5df2-9480-36d57f4806a3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527205 #edb/ref 1074 "Recall that if $X$ and $Y$ are random variables, then\n\n$$\n\\text{Var}[X\\pm Y]=\\text{Var}[X]+\\text{Var}[Y]\\pm 2\\text{Cov}[X,Y].\n$$\n\nSubstituting the given data into the formula above, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\text{Var}[X+Y]=\\frac{1}{8}+\\frac{3}{4}+2\\cdot \\frac{5}{16}=\\frac{3}{2}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527208 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186527092 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527208 #edb/ref 1069 "q-148304" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527208 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527208 #edb/ref 1071 "For two random variables $X$ and $Y,$ we have $\\text{Var}[X]=0.64,$ $\\text{Var}[Y]=2.56,$ and $\\text{Cov}[X,Y]=0.48.$ Find $\\text{Var}[X+Y].$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527208 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "c3e39928-eadb-5eb3-9eda-f3457f0505b4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527208 #edb/ref 1074 "Recall that if $X$ and $Y$ are random variables, then\n\n$$\n\\text{Var}[X\\pm Y]=\\text{Var}[X]+\\text{Var}[Y]\\pm 2\\text{Cov}[X,Y].\n$$\n\nSubstituting the given data into the formula above, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\text{Var}[X+Y]=0.64+2.56+2\\cdot 0.48=4.16.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527210 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186527047 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527210 #edb/ref 1069 "q-148329" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527210 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527210 #edb/ref 1071 "For two random variables $X$ and $Y,$ we have $\\text{Var}[X]=3,$ $\\text{Var}[Y]=5,$ and $\\text{Cov}[X,Y]=1.$ Find $\\text{Var}[3X-4Y].$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527210 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "d139a777-1244-5988-9682-4e7da954d933" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527210 #edb/ref 1074 "Recall that if $X$ and $Y$ are random variables, and $a$ and $b$ are constants, then\n\n$$\n\\text{Var}[aX+bY]={a}^{2}\\text{Var}[X]+{b}^{2}\\text{Var}[Y]+2ab\\,\\text{Cov}[X,Y].\n$$\n\nSubstituting the given data into the formula above, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[3X-4Y] & ={3}^{2}\\cdot 3+(-4)^{2}\\cdot 5+2\\cdot 3\\cdot (-4)\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =27+80-24 \\\\  & =83.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527219 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186527068 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527219 #edb/ref 1069 "q-148399" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527219 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527219 #edb/ref 1071 "For three random variables ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},$ and ${X}_{3},$ we have the following variances and covariances:\n\n$$\n\\text{Var}[{X}_{1}]=3,\\,\\text{Var}[{X}_{2}]=0.5,\\,\\text{Var}[{X}_{3}]=1.5\\,\n$$\n\n$$\n\\text{Cov}[{X}_{1},{X}_{2}]=-0.75,\\,\\text{Cov}[{X}_{1},{X}_{3}]=-3.5,\\,\\text{Cov}[{X}_{2},{X}_{3}]=4\\,\n$$\n\nFind $\\text{Var}[{X}_{1}+4{X}_{2}+6{X}_{3}].$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527219 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "e0b47f27-b72b-520b-9e5b-9f7678a818fc" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527219 #edb/ref 1074 "Recall that if ${X}_{1},…,{X}_{n}$ are random variables, and ${a}_{1},…,{a}_{n}$ are constants, then\n\n$$\n\\text{Var}[{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{a}_{i}{X}_{i}]={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{a}_{i}^{2}\\text{Var}[{X}_{i}]+2{\\sum }_{i<j}{a}_{i}{a}_{j}\\text{Cov}[{X}_{i},{X}_{j}].\n$$\n\nHence, for $n=3$ random variables, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[{a}_{1}{X}_{1}+{a}_{2}{X}_{2}+{a}_{3}{X}_{3}] & ={a}_{1}^{2}\\text{Var}[{X}_{1}]+{a}_{2}^{2}\\text{Var}[{X}_{2}]+{a}_{3}^{2}\\text{Var}[{X}_{3}] \\\\  & +2{a}_{1}{a}_{2}\\text{Cov}[{X}_{1},{X}_{2}]+2{a}_{1}{a}_{3}\\text{Cov}[{X}_{1},{X}_{3}]+2{a}_{2}{a}_{3}\\text{Cov}[{X}_{2},{X}_{3}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the given data into the formula above, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[{X}_{1}+4{X}_{2}+6{X}_{3}] & ={1}^{2}\\cdot 3+{4}^{2}\\cdot 0.5+{6}^{2}\\cdot 1.5 \\\\  & +2\\cdot 1\\cdot 4\\cdot (-0.75)+2\\cdot 1\\cdot 6\\cdot (-3.5)+2\\cdot 4\\cdot 6\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =3+8+54-6-42+192 \\\\  & =209.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527224 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186527111 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527224 #edb/ref 1069 "q-148348" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527224 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527224 #edb/ref 1071 "The joint probability mass function of the random variables $X$ and $Y$ is given below.\n\n| $f$ | $Y=1$ | $Y=2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=0$ | $0.3$ | $0.25$ |\n| $X=2$ | $0.2$ | $0.25$ |\n\nFind $\\text{Var}[3X-2Y].$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527224 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "e96228aa-7e46-5ce5-96d2-d22e87559f00" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527224 #edb/ref 1074 "Recall that if $X$ and $Y$ are random variables and $a$ and $b$ are constants, then\n\n$$\n\\text{Var}[aX+bY]={a}^{2}\\text{Var}[X]+{b}^{2}\\text{Var}[Y]+2ab\\,\\text{Cov}[X,Y].\n$$\n\nAlso, the variance of a random variable $X$ is determined by the formula\n\n$$\n\\text{Var}[X]=\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]-\\text{E}[X]^{2},\n$$\n\nand the covariance of two random variables $X$ and $Y$ is given by the formula\n\n$$\n\\text{Cov}[X,Y]=\\text{E}[XY]-\\text{E}[X]\\cdot \\text{E}[Y].\n$$\n\nFirst, let's find the marginal mass functions for $X$ and $Y.$ These are given by the row and column totals, respectively.\n\n| $f$ | $Y=1$ | $Y=2$ | ${f}_{X}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=0$ | $0.3$ | $0.25$ | $0.55$ |\n| $X=2$ | $0.2$ | $0.25$ | $0.45$ |\n| ${f}_{Y}$ | $0.5$ | $0.5$ | $1$ |\n\nNow, we can find $\\text{Var}[X].$ If $X$ is defined on a sample space ${S}_{X},$ then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[X] & ={\\sum }_{x\\in {S}_{X}}x\\cdot {f}_{X}(x) \\\\  & =0\\cdot 0.55+2\\cdot 0.45 \\\\  & =0.9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[{X}^{2}] & ={\\sum }_{x\\in {S}_{X}}{x}^{2}\\cdot {f}_{X}(x) \\\\  & ={0}^{2}\\cdot 0.55+{2}^{2}\\cdot 0.45 \\\\  & =1.8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[X] & =\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]-\\text{E}[X]^{2} \\\\  & =1.8-{0.9}^{2} \\\\  & =0.99.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, if $Y$ is defined on a sample space ${S}_{Y},$ then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[Y] & ={\\sum }_{y\\in {S}_{y}}y\\cdot {f}_{Y}(y) \\\\  & =1\\cdot 0.5+2\\cdot 0.5 \\\\  & =1.5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[{Y}^{2}] & ={\\sum }_{y\\in {S}_{y}}{y}^{2}\\cdot {f}_{Y}(y) \\\\  & ={1}^{2}\\cdot 0.5+{2}^{2}\\cdot 0.5 \\\\  & =2.5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[Y] & =\\text{E}[{Y}^{2}]-\\text{E}[Y]^{2} \\\\  & =2.5-{1.5}^{2} \\\\  & =0.25.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's find $\\text{Cov}[X,Y].$ We find $\\text{E}[XY]$ using the rule of the lazy statistician:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[XY] & ={\\sum }_{(x,y)\\in S}xy\\cdot f(x,y) \\\\  & =0\\cdot 1\\cdot f(0,1)+0\\cdot 2\\cdot f(0,2)+2\\cdot 1\\cdot f(2,1)+2\\cdot 2\\cdot f(2,2) \\\\  & =0+0+2\\cdot 0.2+4\\cdot 0.25 \\\\  & =1.4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, using the covariance formula, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Cov}[XY] & =\\text{E}[XY]-\\text{E}[X]\\cdot \\text{E}[Y] \\\\  & =1.4-0.9\\cdot 1.5 \\\\  & =0.05.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[3X-2Y] & ={3}^{2}\\cdot 0.99+(-2)^{2}\\cdot 0.25+2\\cdot 3\\cdot (-2)\\cdot 0.05 \\\\  & =8.91+1-0.6 \\\\  & =9.31.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
